<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sutton Hoo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sutton Hoo is a site near Woodbridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with a cemetery of early 7th-century burial mounds. Its great ship burial, excavated in 1939, held a helmet, a gold buckle, shoulder clasps and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the Byzantine world, and is widely thought to be the grave of Rædwald, king of the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, who died around 624. The finds are the richest evidence for the wealth and contacts of East Anglia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo.jpg" alt="Sutton Hoo" width="320" height="119" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Healing · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutton_Hoo_burial_site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
